  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Нет, нет, ничего страшного.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "А где ваше главное начальство?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And where is your top management?' the guest brings out the contrast between 'and' and 'where' in a few short words. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Ждём её через пару часов.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We are expecting her in a couple of hours.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'we await' before landing on 'hours'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Это очень вкусная пицца.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'This is a very delicious pizza.' the guest brings out the contrast between 'this' and 'very' in a few short words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Прямо как я люблю.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Just the way I love it.' the guest anchors the line on 'right', while 'like' completes the thought.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Видно, что делали профессионалы.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'It is clear that professionals made it.' the guest anchors the line on 'one can see', while 'professionals' completes the thought.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Ну, мы очень старались.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Well, we tried very hard.' In everyday Russian, 'very' flows straight into 'we tried hard' without extra linking words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Дома там невкусно готовят.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'At home they cook badly.' In Russian, the idea of 'at home' flows naturally into 'to cook' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Скажи шефу, чтобы написал рецепт.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Tell the chef to write down the recipe.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'say' before landing on 'recipe'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "elena",
   "t": "Витя, всё очень вкусно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Vitya, everything is very delicious.' Notice how Russian expresses 'proper name' and 'deliciously' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "elena",
   "t": "Спасибо, что ты меня пригласил.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Thank you for inviting me.' Elena addresses the listener with the informal singular 'you' alongside 'invited'. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Здесь ты попробуешь французскую кухню.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Here you will taste French cuisine.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'here' and 'you' in a few short words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "elena",
   "t": "Нет, спасибо, я пойду.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'No, thank you, I will go.' Elena anchors the line on 'no', while 'will go' completes the thought.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Нет, нет, ничего страшного.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'No, no, nothing is wrong.' The grammar packs 'no' and 'terrible' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
